1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,730
When you were eight, a single
summer holiday lasted approximately forever.

2
00:00:03,730 --> 00:00:04,900
Six weeks.

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:07,420
You'd be a slightly different person
by the end of it.

4
00:00:07,420 --> 00:00:11,190
The first week alone contained more
than some entire years do now.

5
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:12,650
And this year?

6
00:00:12,650 --> 00:00:17,570
This year has been going for a while and
you couldn't confidently tell me what happened in March.

7
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:19,660
Everyone notices this.

8
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:22,050
Everyone assumes it's just a feeling.

9
00:00:22,050 --> 00:00:23,740
It isn't just a feeling.

10
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:30,570
There are two competing explanations, both of them are partly right, and
one of them tells you exactly what to do about it.

11
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:36,160
First, let's kill the obvious theory, because
it's wrong and it gets repeated constantly.

12
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:41,820
The idea is that your internal clock slows down as
you age, so external time seems to whip past.

13
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:44,520
Researchers have tested this directly.

14
00:00:44,520 --> 00:00:50,050
Sit people of different ages in a room and ask them
to estimate when three minutes have passed, with no clock.

15
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:53,270
Older adults do this about
as well as younger ones.

16
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:59,430
There's some variation, but nothing remotely big enough to explain the
difference between a summer at eight and a summer at forty.

17
00:00:59,430 --> 00:01:05,930
And here's the detail that really rules it out: in
the moment, older people don't experience time as fast.

18
00:01:05,930 --> 00:01:11,720
Ask someone how long this afternoon felt, and
you get roughly normal answers at every age.

19
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:14,180
The distortion isn't happening live.

20
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:16,140
It's happening in the rear-view mirror.

21
00:01:16,140 --> 00:01:19,200
Which means whatever's going on
isn't in your clock.

22
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:20,470
It's in your memory.

23
00:01:20,470 --> 00:01:27,080
The oldest explanation is also the simplest, and it
comes from a nineteenth-century philosopher called Paul Janet.

24
00:01:27,080 --> 00:01:32,220
His idea: you judge a length of time
against the total amount of time you've lived.

25
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:35,300
When you're one year old, a
year is your entire life.

26
00:01:35,300 --> 00:01:36,130
Everything.

27
00:01:36,130 --> 00:01:41,060
When you're five, a year is a fifth
of everything you've ever known — still enormous.

28
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:42,540
At ten, a tenth.

29
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:44,700
At forty, one fortieth.

30
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:47,250
Same three hundred and sixty-five days.

31
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:49,280
Wildly different fraction of the file.

32
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:53,470
Run that logic forward and
it produces an unpleasant result.

33
00:01:53,470 --> 00:02:01,650
If you measure life in how long it subjectively feels rather than how many
years it contains, the halfway point of your life arrives at about age seven.

34
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:04,870
By twenty, you've subjectively used
up most of it.

35
00:02:04,870 --> 00:02:09,230
Now, this is elegant, and
it's clearly capturing something real.

36
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:12,470
But it can't be the whole
story, and here's how you know.

37
00:02:12,470 --> 00:02:18,970
The proportional theory says the speed-up should be smooth
and constant, a gentle gradient across your whole life.

38
00:02:18,970 --> 00:02:19,890
It isn't.

39
00:02:19,890 --> 00:02:24,200
People report specific stretches that flew
and specific stretches that dragged.

40
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:28,330
Some people have a year in their thirties
that felt longer than the five around it.

41
00:02:28,330 --> 00:02:32,730
And almost everybody had one very
recent year that behaved extremely strangely.

42
00:02:32,730 --> 00:02:36,620
So here's the second explanation, and
I think it's the better one.

43
00:02:36,620 --> 00:02:42,750
Your sense of how long a period of time was
is reconstructed afterwards from how much you remember of it.

44
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:47,130
A stretch stuffed with distinct,
retrievable memories reads as long.

45
00:02:47,130 --> 00:02:53,600
A stretch that produced almost nothing readable reads as short —
even if it was exactly the same number of days.

46
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:57,590
And your brain is extremely selective
about what it bothers to record.

47
00:02:57,590 --> 00:02:59,210
It records novelty.

48
00:02:59,210 --> 00:03:03,870
New places, new people, first times,
things that broke the pattern.

49
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:09,240
It does not bother recording the fourth thousand
identical commute, because there's no reason to.

50
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:11,320
Now think about being a child.

51
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:13,190
Everything is the first time.

52
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:19,600
First time on a bike, first time at the sea, first day at
a new school, first time you understood a joke an adult made.

53
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:23,440
A child's year is almost entirely
composed of things worth filing.

54
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:25,600
And think about a typical adult year.

55
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:30,800
Same job, same route, same flat,
same faces, same weekly shop.

56
00:03:30,800 --> 00:03:32,930
The days were not shorter.

57
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:35,600
There were just far fewer
of them worth keeping.

58
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:37,750
Try it on yourself right now.

59
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:42,820
Think back to a holiday you took somewhere
unfamiliar — a week, maybe ten days.

60
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:45,880
You can probably produce five or
six distinct scenes from it.

61
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:47,360
A specific meal.

62
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:48,520
Getting lost.

63
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:49,960
A conversation.

64
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:51,910
The way a particular street looked.

65
00:03:51,910 --> 00:03:55,730
Now think back to a random
ordinary week from four months ago.

66
00:03:55,730 --> 00:03:57,310
Same seven days.

67
00:03:57,310 --> 00:04:04,270
Almost certainly you cannot retrieve a single specific scene from
it, and if you can, it's because something unusual happened.

68
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:06,630
Your brain is not being lazy.

69
00:04:06,630 --> 00:04:07,860
It's being efficient.

70
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:10,070
There was nothing there that it needed.

71
00:04:10,070 --> 00:04:12,460
That's why the summer
at eight feels enormous.

72
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:14,060
It isn't nostalgia.

73
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:17,380
That summer genuinely left
more material behind.

74
00:04:17,380 --> 00:04:22,920
And then, a few years ago, the
entire planet accidentally ran the experiment.

75
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:33,920
During the lockdowns of twenty twenty, researchers surveyed thousands of people about their sense of
time, and got a result that looks contradictory until you understand the two-system explanation.

76
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:37,380
Enormous numbers of people reported
that the days crawled.

77
00:04:37,380 --> 00:04:38,590
Interminable.

78
00:04:38,590 --> 00:04:42,590
Nothing to do, nothing happening, an
afternoon that would not end.

79
00:04:42,590 --> 00:04:45,660
And the same people reported
that the months had vanished.

80
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:47,850
Where did the year go?

81
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:48,870
What did I do?

82
00:04:48,870 --> 00:04:49,730
It's gone.

83
00:04:49,730 --> 00:04:53,870
Slow days, fast months, at the
same time, in the same person.

84
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:56,460
That's exactly what
memory density predicts.

85
00:04:56,460 --> 00:05:03,110
In the moment, an empty day drags, because there is nothing
to occupy attention and you notice every minute of it.

86
00:05:03,110 --> 00:05:07,810
But afterwards, that same empty day
left nothing behind to measure with.

87
00:05:07,810 --> 00:05:12,250
Two months of identical afternoons collapse
into a single vague blur.

88
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:18,440
Live time and remembered time are not the same
thing, and they can move in opposite directions.

89
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:24,420
Which is the key to the whole video: the reason your
years are speeding up is not that they're going faster.

90
00:05:24,420 --> 00:05:26,600
It's that they're leaving less behind.

91
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:31,810
Put the two theories together and you can see why
the effect hits hardest in a very specific window.

92
00:05:31,810 --> 00:05:35,840
Childhood: high novelty,
small denominator.

93
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,150
Time is vast.

94
00:05:37,150 --> 00:05:40,510
Late teens and twenties:
still very high novelty.

95
00:05:40,510 --> 00:05:46,230
First job, first move, first relationship,
first time being responsible for yourself.

96
00:05:46,230 --> 00:05:58,180
There's a well-documented thing in memory research called the reminiscence bump — when older adults are asked
to recall their lives, a disproportionate number of vivid memories come from roughly fifteen to thirty.

97
00:05:58,180 --> 00:06:01,680
That period is over-represented for
the rest of your life.

98
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:04,410
Then, for most people, something changes.

99
00:06:04,410 --> 00:06:06,260
The big firsts run out.

100
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:08,770
Life becomes competent and repeated.

101
00:06:08,770 --> 00:06:19,390
And competence is the enemy here, because the whole point of becoming good at something is that you
can do it without paying attention — and anything you did without paying attention was not written down.

102
00:06:19,390 --> 00:06:20,980
That's the acceleration.

103
00:06:20,980 --> 00:06:21,920
Not aging.

104
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:23,340
Repetition.

105
00:06:23,340 --> 00:06:29,110
Which is oddly hopeful, because repetition is a
thing you can change and aging isn't.

106
00:06:29,110 --> 00:06:34,730
So if you want your years back, the instruction
that falls out of this is annoyingly specific.

107
00:06:34,730 --> 00:06:40,880
It is not "slow down." It's not mindfulness,
or being present, though those are fine things.

108
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:44,070
Being present makes today feel
longer while you're in it.

109
00:06:44,070 --> 00:06:46,660
It doesn't necessarily
leave anything behind.

110
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:51,090
The instruction is: create memorable first
times, and space them out.

111
00:06:51,090 --> 00:06:57,300
A weekend somewhere you have never been will do more to lengthen
your year than a fortnight in the place you go every year.

112
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:01,290
The unfamiliar route home leaves a
mark that the usual one doesn't.

113
00:07:01,290 --> 00:07:09,010
A new skill, badly done, generates enormous amounts of storable
material precisely because you're bad at it and paying attention.

114
00:07:09,010 --> 00:07:13,270
And the second part matters as much
as the first: spread them out.

115
00:07:13,270 --> 00:07:20,770
Six new things across twelve months lengthens a year far more than
six new things in one week, because they act as markers.

116
00:07:20,770 --> 00:07:25,090
Your brain measures duration partly by
counting the distance between landmarks.

117
00:07:25,090 --> 00:07:29,330
Bunch the landmarks together and most
of the year is featureless again.

118
00:07:29,330 --> 00:07:31,360
The advice is boring.

119
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:33,000
The mechanism is not.

120
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,400
You are not trying to enjoy yourself more.

121
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:37,140
You are trying to leave evidence.

122
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:40,560
There's a harder implication in here and
I'd rather say it than dodge it.

123
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:52,680
If the length of your life, as you'll eventually experience it looking back, is a function of how much of it you
actually recorded — then a life can be long in years and short in the only currency that ends up mattering.

124
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:55,960
Twenty years of the same
week is not twenty years.

125
00:07:55,960 --> 00:07:57,220
Not in memory.

126
00:07:57,220 --> 00:08:01,080
It compresses down to something you
could describe in a paragraph.

127
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:02,890
And nobody chooses that.

128
00:08:02,890 --> 00:08:11,180
It happens by accumulation, one reasonable comfortable decision at a time, until the
pattern is so smooth there's nothing left for the brain to catch on.

129
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:14,560
That's the real reason the summer at
eight feels the way it does.

130
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:17,020
It isn't that you were happier.

131
00:08:17,020 --> 00:08:24,620
It's that you were paying attention, constantly, because you had no choice — everything
was new and you had no idea what was going to happen next.

132
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:27,290
So no, time isn't speeding up.

133
00:08:27,290 --> 00:08:28,550
Your clock is fine.

134
00:08:28,550 --> 00:08:34,660
What's changed is how much of each year survives the trip into memory,
and that's a function of how much of it was worth recording.

135
00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:39,650
The good news is that this is one of
the very few things about aging that pushes back.

136
00:08:39,650 --> 00:08:42,480
You cannot slow the calendar.

137
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:44,790
You can absolutely change the density.

138
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:46,890
Go somewhere you've never been.

139
00:08:46,890 --> 00:08:48,730
Be bad at something in public.

140
00:08:48,730 --> 00:08:50,780
Take the wrong road home once in a while.

141
00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:55,130
Your brain is keeping a record, and it only writes
down the parts where it wasn't sure what was coming.

142
00:08:55,130 --> 00:09:02,280
And if you're now wondering how much of last year you
could actually reconstruct if somebody asked...
...that's a question for another video.
